We have exceptionally strong **current 2026 hospital-specific evidence** that prolonged ED boarding exists and that system-level causes dominate the delayed-transfer cohort.

We do **not** have direct evidence that AIIMS Bhopal currently lacks:

- real-time bed visibility;
- transfer-case tracking;
- speciality acceptance tracking;
- owner assignment;
- ageing alerts;
- escalation;
- closed-loop transfer monitoring.

Therefore this checkpoint should **not** be marked as fully validated.

---

Of the 438 patients who eventually transferred:

- 171 stayed 24–<48 hours;
- 139 stayed 48–<72 hours;
- 98 stayed 72–<96 hours;
- **30 stayed ≥96 hours**.

Thus prolonged transfer is not merely a short handoff delay. There is a significant long-aging tail.

The study recommends:

- hospital-wide bed dashboards;
- dedicated surge ICU beds;
- clear routing protocols;
- ED physician authority to transfer >24-hour boarders after consultation.

These recommendations are evidence that the authors see system-level flow coordination as an intervention surface.

---

The study does **not** say:

> “AIIMS Bhopal currently has no transfer-management software.”

It does not establish:

- whether a live bed dashboard exists in 2026;
- whether bed data are integrated across units;
- whether speciality acceptance is digitally tracked;
- whether an overdue transfer automatically escalates;
- whether a central transfer queue exists;
- how many calls/messages staff make per case;
- how much staff time is spent resolving each exception.

These remain **UNKNOWN**.

This distinction is essential.

---

The same broad transfer problem is independently supported by Indian evidence.

A prospective ED-to-ICU study reported:

- **52** critically ill ED-to-ICU admissions;
- **35 (67.3%)** not transferred within 30 minutes;
- mean transfer time **69.9 ± 62.5 minutes**;
- ED-related factors accounted for **54.2%** of delays;
- regular feedback reduced transfer time by **42.6%** during the study period.

This supports the broader industry problem.

However:

> **Feedback-based improvement is not evidence that automation is currently missing.**

It only strengthens the recurrence of transfer-delay pain.

